# .dockerignore — repo root
#
# docker/worker/Dockerfile builds with the repo root as its build context
# (so `COPY dist/loom-daemon-<target>` lines up with the exact path
# `.github/workflows/release.yml`'s build-daemon job already packages
# release assets under). This repo has a large working tree (Rust
# target/, git history, node_modules, per-issue worktrees, …) that the
# Dockerfile never references, so deny-all-then-allowlist keeps the
# context sent to the docker daemon small and free of anything that could
# accidentally leak into a layer via a future wildcard COPY.
#
# If a future Dockerfile in this repo needs another path from the repo
# root, add an explicit `!`-allowlist entry for it here — do not widen this
# to an allow-all.
*
!dist/
!dist/**
!docker/
!docker/**
# The private-session control bundle (issue #8839): docker/session/Dockerfile
# stages these exact files into an image-owned, digest-sealed
# /opt/loom/private-control/ so a private Codex session's guard code, the
# libraries those guards source, and the hook provisioner all live OUTSIDE the
# worker-writable clone. Enumerated file-by-file rather than as `!defaults/**`
# on purpose: nothing else under defaults/ (roles, docs, slash commands,
# runtimes) belongs in a session image, and an allow-all here would let a
# future wildcard COPY pull it in silently. Every entry below is also named in
# bundle.rs's sealed asset list, and `seal-control` fails the build if one is
# missing — so a stale allowlist entry cannot degrade into a bundle that ships
# a guard without the library it sources.
!defaults/
!defaults/hooks/guard-codex-bridge.sh
!defaults/hooks/guard-destructive-generic.sh
!defaults/hooks/guard-loom-workflow.sh
!defaults/hooks/guard-worktree-paths.sh
!defaults/scripts/provision-codex-hooks.sh
!defaults/scripts/lib/canonical-path.sh
!defaults/scripts/lib/config-resolver.sh
!defaults/scripts/lib/default-branch.sh
!defaults/scripts/lib/installed-file-guard.sh
!defaults/scripts/lib/worktree-root.sh
